# Dataset Benchmark Framework

A declarative, validation-first ETL pipeline designed to ingest, audit, and clean public data repositories into standardized, legally clear, and highly resilient modeling assets for automated machine learning benchmarking.

## Overview

In production and benchmarking environments, machine learning pipelines frequently crash due to unexpected schema shifts, missing target spaces, or uncontrolled class imbalance. This project addresses these challenges by enforcing a strict definition of a "Good Dataset" through multi-layer verification gates before any modeling code is run. 

This framework replaces haphazard manual data gathering with an automated Python pipeline inspired by the rigorous data-integrity standards of the OpenML CC-18 benchmark suite.

## Educational Context & Learning Outcomes

This project was developed as a comprehensive engineering assignment to demonstrate end-to-end data platform development. 
The core educational and engineering objectives achieved in this repository include:

* **Data Engineering & ETL:** Building robust extraction, transformation, and loading pipelines using `pandas`.
* **API Integration:** Interacting with the OpenML API to programmatically search, filter, and stream external datasets.
* **Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) Parsing:** Designing a custom query parser to translate human-readable string filters (e.g., `n_instances >= 1000 AND task == classification`) into executable configuration contracts.
* **Data Validation & Cleaning:** Applying strict algorithmic data quality constraints (removing high-cardinality leakage, handling missing data, ensuring target integrity) prior to model ingestion.
* **System Architecture:** Decoupling system components into distinct layers (Definition -> Ingestion -> Cataloging -> Validation -> Export) for maintainability and modularity.

## AI Acknowledgment & Development Process

As a modern engineering project, Large Language Models (LLMs) were utilized as a pair-programming tool during the development of this framework. While the overarching system architecture, data engineering pipeline, and validation logic were designed independently, AI assistance was heavily leveraged to review the code and implement the most complex algorithmic components of the repository.

Building a recursive descent tokenizer and parser to translate raw string queries into structured YAML blueprints involves complex computer science and compiler theory. Using AI to help write and implement this specific module allowed me to successfully integrate a highly advanced feature into the framework. This pragmatic approach cleared a major technical bottleneck, allowing me to focus my efforts on the broader data engineering, pipeline architecture, and OpenML integration.

## Architecture & Features

The pipeline operates as a sequence of independent components where the output of one engine serves as the strict operational contract for the next.

1. **Definition (`define_collection.py`):** Uses a custom AST parser to compile text filter queries into structured YAML blueprints.
2. **Ingestion (`download_collection.py`):** Converts bounds into remote API queries, profiles active memory streams, and enforces license/citation lookups via OpenML.
3. **Cataloging (`build_catalog.py`):** Conducts structural diagnostics across downloaded raw CSVs, infers machine learning task types, and builds a centralized CSV ledger.
4. **Sanitization (`validate_datasets.py`):** Executes physical row/column drops, eliminates constant/leakage features, ensures statistical validity, and materializes frozen `.resolved.yaml` manifests.
5. **Deployment (`export_collections.py`):** Packages validated matrices into clean folder hierarchies or zip archives ready for external Jupyter environments or model training scripts.

## Installation

Ensure you have Python 3.12+ installed. Clone the repository and install in editable mode with development dependencies:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/your-username/dataset-benchmark-framework.git
cd dataset-benchmark-framework
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

This installs the `benchmark-framework` console command and all dev tooling (pytest, ruff, mypy).

## Quick Start

The pipeline runs as a sequence of stages (define → download → catalog → validate → export). You can run individual stages or the full pipeline:

```bash
# Define a collection blueprint
benchmark-framework define --filter "task == classification" --id my_collection --name "My Collection"

# Download datasets from OpenML
benchmark-framework download --collection my_collection --limit 5

# Catalog, validate, and export
benchmark-framework catalog
benchmark-framework validate
benchmark-framework export --collection my_collection --format zip
```

Or run the entire pipeline at once:

```bash
benchmark-framework run --filter "task == classification" --id my_collection --limit 5
```

Each stage script can also be called directly from `scripts/`:

```bash
python scripts/run_pipeline.py full --filter "task == classification" --id smoke_test --collection smoke_test --limit 2
```

## Demo

The project includes an offline demo that generates deterministic synthetic data and exercises the full pipeline without any network access or API credentials:

```bash
# Run the demo (cleans up temporary files automatically)
python -m pipeline.demo

# Keep temporary files for inspection
python -m pipeline.demo --keep

# Via the Makefile
make demo
```

The demo creates two synthetic datasets (one classification, one regression), catalogs them, validates/cleans them, and exports a folder archive — all in a temporary directory.

## CLI

The `benchmark-framework` command provides subcommands for each pipeline stage:

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `define` | Define a collection blueprint from filter expressions |
| `download` | Download datasets from OpenML for a collection |
| `catalog` | Build the dataset catalog from downloaded raw CSVs |
| `validate` | Validate and clean cataloged datasets |
| `export` | Export validated collections (ZIP or folder, optional Parquet) |
| `run` | Run the full pipeline in sequence |
| `demo` | Run the offline demo |

Each command supports `--help` for detailed usage.

## Testing

The test suite uses `pytest` and runs without network access. OpenML is mocked in all tests:

```bash
# Run the full test suite
pytest

# Run a specific test file
pytest tests/test_demo.py -v

# Via the Makefile
make test

# Full CI-equivalent checks
make ci
```

### Makefile Commands

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `make install` | Install runtime dependencies |
| `make dev-install` | Install the project + all dev dependencies in editable mode |
| `make test` | Run the test suite |
| `make lint` | Run ruff linter |
| `make typecheck` | Run mypy type checker |
| `make ci` | Run lint + typecheck + test |
| `make demo` | Run the offline demo with `--keep` |
| `make pipeline` | Run the offline demo without keeping temp files |
| `make clean` | Remove build artifacts and caches |
